WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.730
Good morning, Hank, it's Fri- Blue guys, what
are you doing lyin' around on the job? Show

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:06.060
some respect for my audience.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.060
That's better.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:09.310
So Hank, I've been sick lately - in fact,
I have a note from my doctor saying I don't

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:12.920
have to do my punishment - but I wanted to
continue our conversation about religion.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.059
But I also know that some people find that
kinda boring, so here's some dancing, dancing,

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:19.100
dancing, dancing, dancing, dance, dance, dance,
dance!

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.750
Right, so Hank, in your video on Wednesday,
you pointed out how difficult it can be to

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:26.060
answer yes or no questions like "Do you believe
in God?" because you have to define terms

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:29.939
like "believe" and "God." Also, "in." And
that made me think about how difficult it

00:00:29.939 --> 00:00:32.070
is to define any religious identity.

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:36.051
Like, for instance, Muslims - Muslims believe
in the Five Pillars of Islam, right? You have

00:00:36.051 --> 00:00:39.829
the shahada, the belief that there is no God
but God; prayer, at least five times a day.

00:00:39.829 --> 00:00:43.800
Then you have almsgiving or zakat, the pilgrimage
to Mecca, and there's a fifth one - Ramadan.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.380
Right, so those are the Five Pillars of Islam,
the foundation of Islamic belief. Except the

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:51.550
majority of Muslims don't follow the Five
Pillars of Islam; for instance, the majority

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.399
of Muslims don't pray five times a day.

00:00:53.399 --> 00:00:57.370
Similarly, many Jews don't practice kosher,
many Buddhists disagree radically about the

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:00.960
nature of Nirvana and the path to get there,
and many Christians think other Christians

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.140
are going to hell because they believe that
Jesus visited America or because they believe

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.219
in transubstantiation, or all kinds of things.

00:01:06.219 --> 00:01:09.390
In fact Hank, one time when I was in college,
I visited a Pentecostal church in rural Florida,

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:12.869
and it was really interesting. There was this
one moment when a woman said to the pastor,

00:01:12.869 --> 00:01:17.229
"Could you pray for my sister? She's on this
horrible path to damnation. She's completely

00:01:17.229 --> 00:01:21.299
lost." And the pastor was like, "Yeah, what's
the problem?" And the woman was like, "She's

00:01:21.299 --> 00:01:24.810
going to the Pentecostal church across the
street." And then the pastor was like, "Oh

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:25.939
yeah, that is a problem."

00:01:25.939 --> 00:01:29.709
So even if you open up all the matryoshka
dolls, even within a particular denomination

00:01:29.709 --> 00:01:34.029
of a particular sect of a particular religious
tradition, there are still big disagreements.

00:01:34.029 --> 00:01:37.220
I mean, one of the fascinating things about
identifying yourself within a religious tradition

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:41.579
is that, no matter who you are, there will
always be people within that very religious

00:01:41.579 --> 00:01:44.869
tradition who say that you are not Jewish
or Muslim or Christian or whatever.

00:01:44.869 --> 00:01:49.060
Okay, this is getting a little serious. Let's
dance. Dance, dance, dancin', dance, dance

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:50.369
dance dance, dance, dance!

00:01:50.369 --> 00:01:53.840
This makes it incredibly challenging to define
the word "Christian" or "Muslim" or "Jew,"

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:58.149
because ultimately, there are Muslims who
I have more theologically in common with than

00:01:58.149 --> 00:02:01.029
many of my fellow Christians. And I think
that's one of the reasons religious traditions

00:02:01.029 --> 00:02:05.429
often look back to some point in history where
it seems like things weren't as fuzzy as they

00:02:05.429 --> 00:02:09.819
are now. Like in Islam there's the Age of
the Golden Caliphate - the first four caliphs;

00:02:09.819 --> 00:02:13.909
in Christianity there's, you know, Paul and
the initial apostles when Christianity was

00:02:13.909 --> 00:02:17.069
first starting to grow; in Judaism you have
the rule of King David. But the truth is that

00:02:17.069 --> 00:02:21.130
there was never a golden age, and certainly
there's no way to recapture the golden age

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:25.260
by trying to go back to it. We know this,
Hank, due to thousands of years of failure.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:29.100
So how do you know, for instance, if you're
a nerdfighter? You as a nerdfighter are constantly

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:33.730
inventing and evolving the definition of that
word. So please don't forget to be awesome.

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:35.379
Hank, I will see you on Monday.

